ligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." 13194139536171 17592186045443 false] [17592186135142 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\hat{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value. The residuals for the data set we considered earlier are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/515aaaeef2f85bb131564b92bdaef9ef514d05c8028ae85382d231565d490665.png)\n\nIn the past, you may have seen that  calculating a regression line typically means finding a line where the sum of the squared residuals \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nUsing matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the condition that the sum of the squared residuals is minimized is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.$ This, in turn, is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.$ \n\nTherefore, the problem of minimizing the squared residuals is equivalent to solving the least squares problem for $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$" 13194139536171 17592186045443 true] [17592186135142 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value. The residuals for the data set we considered earlier are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/515aaaeef2f85bb131564b92bdaef9ef514d05c8028ae85382d231565d490665.png)\n\nIn the past, you may have seen that  calculating a regression line typically means finding a line where the sum of the squared residuals \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nUsing matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the condition that the sum of the squared residuals is minimized is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.$ This, in turn, is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.$ \n\nTherefore, the problem of minimizing the squared residuals is equivalent to solving the least squares problem for $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$" 13194139536171 17592186045443 false] [17592186188207 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to ma