nd $70\\%$ of sports customers change to casual.\n\nCurrently, $700\\,000$ customers purchase casual footwear, while $300\\,000$ customers purchase sports footwear. How many customers will purchase sports footwear in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139536171 17592186045443 true] [17592186378664 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of footwear is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.595 \\\\ 0.405\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $40.5%$ of customers will purchase sports footwear. Computing $40.5%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.405=405\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $405\\,000$ customers will purchase sports footwear in one year." 13194139536171 17592186045443 false] [17592186378664 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of footwear is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\